.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/@eslint/config-array Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "Config Array"
.PP
.SH "Description"
.PP
A config array is a way of managing configurations that are based on glob pattern matching of filenames\&. Each config array contains the information needed to determine the correct configuration for any file based on the filename\&.

.PP
\fBNote:\fP This is a generic package that can be used outside of ESLint\&. It contains no ESLint-specific functionality\&.
.SH "Installation"
.PP
For Node\&.js and compatible runtimes:

.PP
.PP
.nf
npm install @eslint/config\-array
# or
yarn add @eslint/config\-array
# or
pnpm install @eslint/config\-array
# or
bun add @eslint/config\-array
.fi
.PP

.PP
For Deno:

.PP
.PP
.nf
deno add @eslint/config\-array
.fi
.PP
.SH "Background"
.PP
The basic idea is that all configuration, including overrides, can be represented by a single array where each item in the array is a config object\&. Config objects appearing later in the array override config objects appearing earlier in the array\&. You can calculate a config for a given file by traversing all config objects in the array to find the ones that match the filename\&. Matching is done by specifying glob patterns in \fRfiles\fP and \fRignores\fP properties on each config object\&. Here's an example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
export default [
    // match all JSON files
    {
        name: "JSON Handler",
        files: ["**/*\&.json"],
        handler: jsonHandler,
    },

    // match only package\&.json
    {
        name: "package\&.json Handler",
        files: ["package\&.json"],
        handler: packageJsonHandler,
    },
];
.fi
.PP

.PP
In this example, there are two config objects: the first matches all JSON files in all directories and the second matches just \fRpackage\&.json\fP in the base path directory (all the globs are evaluated as relative to a base path that can be specified)\&. When you retrieve a configuration for \fRfoo\&.json\fP, only the first config object matches so \fRhandler\fP is equal to \fRjsonHandler\fP; when you retrieve a configuration for \fRpackage\&.json\fP, \fRhandler\fP is equal to \fRpackageJsonHandler\fP (because both config objects match, the second one wins)\&.
.SH "Usage"
.PP
First, import the \fRConfigArray\fP constructor:

.PP
.PP
.nf
import { ConfigArray } from "@eslint/config\-array";

// or using CommonJS

const { ConfigArray } = require("@eslint/config\-array");
.fi
.PP

.PP
When you create a new instance of \fRConfigArray\fP, you must pass in two arguments: an array of configs and an options object\&. The array of configs is most likely read in from a configuration file, so here's a typical example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const configFilename = path\&.resolve(process\&.cwd(), "my\&.config\&.js");
const { default: rawConfigs } = await import(configFilename);
const configs = new ConfigArray(rawConfigs, {
    // the path to match filenames from
    basePath: process\&.cwd(),

    // additional items in each config
    schema: mySchema,
});
.fi
.PP

.PP
This example reads in an object or array from \fRmy\&.config\&.js\fP and passes it into the \fRConfigArray\fP constructor as the first argument\&. The second argument is an object specifying the \fRbasePath\fP (the directory in which \fRmy\&.config\&.js\fP is found) and a \fRschema\fP to define the additional properties of a config object beyond \fRfiles\fP, \fRignores\fP, \fRbasePath\fP, and \fRname\fP\&.
.SS "Specifying a Schema"
The \fRschema\fP option is required for you to use additional properties in config objects\&. The schema is an object that follows the format of an \fR\fRObjectSchema\fP\fP\&. The schema specifies both validation and merge rules that the \fRConfigArray\fP instance needs to combine configs when there are multiple matches\&. Here's an example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const configFilename = path\&.resolve(process\&.cwd(), "my\&.config\&.js");
const { default: rawConfigs } = await import(configFilename);

const mySchema = {

    // define the handler key in configs
    handler: {
        required: true,
        merge(a, b) {
            if (!b) return a;
            if (!a) return b;
        },
        validate(value) {
            if (typeof value !== "function") {
                throw new TypeError("Function expected\&.");
            }
        }
    }
};

const configs = new ConfigArray(rawConfigs, {

    // the path to match filenames from
    basePath: process\&.cwd(),

    // additional item schemas in each config
    schema: mySchema,

    // additional config types supported (default: [])
    extraConfigTypes: ["array", "function"];
});
.fi
.PP
.SS "Config Arrays"
Config arrays can be multidimensional, so it's possible for a config array to contain another config array when \fRextraConfigTypes\fP contains \fR"array"\fP, such as:

.PP
.PP
.nf
export default [
    // JS config
    {
        files: ["**/*\&.js"],
        handler: jsHandler,
    },

    // JSON configs
    [
        // match all JSON files
        {
            name: "JSON Handler",
            files: ["**/*\&.json"],
            handler: jsonHandler,
        },

        // match only package\&.json
        {
            name: "package\&.json Handler",
            files: ["package\&.json"],
            handler: packageJsonHandler,
        },
    ],

    // filename must match function
    {
        files: [filePath => filePath\&.endsWith("\&.md")],
        handler: markdownHandler,
    },

    // filename must match all patterns in subarray
    {
        files: [["*\&.test\&.*", "*\&.js"]],
        handler: jsTestHandler,
    },

    // filename must not match patterns beginning with !
    {
        name: "Non\-JS files",
        files: ["!*\&.js"],
        settings: {
            js: false,
        },
    },

    // specific settings for files inside `src` directory
    {
        name: "Source files",
        basePath: "src",
        files: ["**/*"],
        settings: {
            source: true,
        },
    },
];
.fi
.PP

.PP
In this example, the array contains both config objects and a config array\&. When a config array is normalized (see details below), it is flattened so only config objects remain\&. However, the order of evaluation remains the same\&.

.PP
If the \fRfiles\fP array contains a function, then that function is called with the path of the file as it was passed in\&. The function is expected to return \fRtrue\fP if there is a match and \fRfalse\fP if not\&. (The \fRignores\fP array can also contain functions\&.)

.PP
If the \fRfiles\fP array contains an item that is an array of strings and functions, then all patterns must match in order for the config to match\&. In the preceding examples, both \fR*\&.test\&.*\fP and \fR*\&.js\fP must match in order for the config object to be used\&.

.PP
If a pattern in the files array begins with \fR!\fP then it excludes that pattern\&. In the preceding example, any filename that doesn't end with \fR\&.js\fP will automatically get a \fRsettings\&.js\fP property set to \fRfalse\fP\&.

.PP
You can also specify an \fRignores\fP key that will force files matching those patterns to not be included\&. If the \fRignores\fP key is in a config object without any other keys, then those ignores will always be applied; otherwise those ignores act as exclusions\&. Here's an example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
export default [

    // Always ignored
    {
        ignores: ["**/\&.git/**", "**/node_modules/**"]
    },

    // \&.eslintrc\&.js file is ignored only when \&.js file matches
    {
        files: ["**/*\&.js"],
        ignores: ["\&.eslintrc\&.js"]
        handler: jsHandler
    }
];
.fi
.PP

.PP
You can use negated patterns in \fRignores\fP to exclude a file that was already ignored, such as:

.PP
.PP
.nf
export default [
    // Ignore all JSON files except tsconfig\&.json
    {
        files: ["**/*"],
        ignores: ["**/*\&.json", "!tsconfig\&.json"],
    },
];
.fi
.PP
.SS "Config Functions"
Config arrays can also include config functions when \fRextraConfigTypes\fP contains \fR"function"\fP\&. A config function accepts a single parameter, \fRcontext\fP (defined by you), and must return either a config object or a config array (it cannot return another function)\&. Config functions allow end users to execute code in the creation of appropriate config objects\&. Here's an example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
export default [
    // JS config
    {
        files: ["**/*\&.js"],
        handler: jsHandler,
    },

    // JSON configs
    function (context) {
        return [
            // match all JSON files
            {
                name: context\&.name + " JSON Handler",
                files: ["**/*\&.json"],
                handler: jsonHandler,
            },

            // match only package\&.json
            {
                name: context\&.name + " package\&.json Handler",
                files: ["package\&.json"],
                handler: packageJsonHandler,
            },
        ];
    },
];
.fi
.PP

.PP
When a config array is normalized, each function is executed and replaced in the config array with the return value\&.

.PP
\fBNote:\fP Config functions can also be async\&.
.SS "Normalizing Config Arrays"
Once a config array has been created and loaded with all of the raw config data, it must be normalized before it can be used\&. The normalization process goes through and flattens the config array as well as executing all config functions to get their final values\&.

.PP
To normalize a config array, call the \fRnormalize()\fP method and pass in a context object:

.PP
.PP
.nf
await configs\&.normalize({
    name: "MyApp",
});
.fi
.PP

.PP
The \fRnormalize()\fP method returns a promise, so be sure to use the \fRawait\fP operator\&. The config array instance is normalized in-place, so you don't need to create a new variable\&.

.PP
If you want to disallow async config functions, you can call \fRnormalizeSync()\fP instead\&. This method is completely synchronous and does not require using the \fRawait\fP operator as it does not return a promise:

.PP
.PP
.nf
await configs\&.normalizeSync({
    name: "MyApp",
});
.fi
.PP

.PP
\fBImportant:\fP Once a \fRConfigArray\fP is normalized, it cannot be changed further\&. You can, however, create a new \fRConfigArray\fP and pass in the normalized instance to create an unnormalized copy\&.
.SS "Getting Config for a File"
To get the config for a file, use the \fRgetConfig()\fP method on a normalized config array and pass in the filename to get a config for:

.PP
.PP
.nf
// pass in filename
const fileConfig = configs\&.getConfig(
    path\&.resolve(process\&.cwd(), "package\&.json"),
);
.fi
.PP

.PP
The config array always returns an object, even if there are no configs matching the given filename\&. You can then inspect the returned config object to determine how to proceed\&.

.PP
A few things to keep in mind:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
If a filename is not an absolute path, it will be resolved relative to the base path directory\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
The returned config object never has \fRfiles\fP, \fRignores\fP, \fRbasePath\fP, or \fRname\fP properties; the only properties on the object will be the other configuration options specified\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
The config array caches configs, so subsequent calls to \fRgetConfig()\fP with the same filename will return in a fast lookup rather than another calculation\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
A config will only be generated if the filename matches an entry in a \fRfiles\fP key\&. A config will not be generated without matching a \fRfiles\fP key (configs without a \fRfiles\fP key are only applied when another config with a \fRfiles\fP key is applied; configs without \fRfiles\fP are never applied on their own)\&. Any config with a \fRfiles\fP key entry that is \fR*\fP or ends with \fR/**\fP or \fR/*\fP will only be applied if another entry in the same \fRfiles\fP key matches or another config matches\&.
.PP
.SH "Determining Ignored Paths"
.PP
You can determine if a file is ignored by using the \fRisFileIgnored()\fP method and passing in the path of any file, as in this example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const ignored = configs\&.isFileIgnored("/foo/bar/baz\&.txt");
.fi
.PP

.PP
A file is considered ignored if any of the following is true:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBIt's parent directory is ignored\&.\fP For example, if \fRfoo\fP is in \fRignores\fP, then \fRfoo/a\&.js\fP is considered ignored\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBIt has an ancestor directory that is ignored\&.\fP For example, if \fRfoo\fP is in \fRignores\fP, then \fRfoo/baz/a\&.js\fP is considered ignored\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBIt matches an ignored file pattern\&.\fP For example, if \fR**/a\&.js\fP is in \fRignores\fP, then \fRfoo/a\&.js\fP and \fRfoo/baz/a\&.js\fP are considered ignored\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBIf it matches an entry in \fRfiles\fP and also in \fRignores\fP\&.\fP For example, if \fR**/*\&.js\fP is in \fRfiles\fP and \fR**/a\&.js\fP is in \fRignores\fP, then \fRfoo/a\&.js\fP and \fRfoo/baz/a\&.js\fP are considered ignored\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBThe file is outside the \fRbasePath\fP\&.\fP If the \fRbasePath\fP is \fR/usr/me\fP, then \fR/foo/a\&.js\fP is considered ignored\&.
.PP

.PP
For directories, use the \fRisDirectoryIgnored()\fP method and pass in the path of any directory, as in this example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const ignored = configs\&.isDirectoryIgnored("/foo/bar/");
.fi
.PP

.PP
A directory is considered ignored if any of the following is true:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBIt's parent directory is ignored\&.\fP For example, if \fRfoo\fP is in \fRignores\fP, then \fRfoo/baz\fP is considered ignored\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBIt has an ancestor directory that is ignored\&.\fP For example, if \fRfoo\fP is in \fRignores\fP, then \fRfoo/bar/baz/a\&.js\fP is considered ignored\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBIt matches and ignored file pattern\&.\fP For example, if \fR**/a\&.js\fP is in \fRignores\fP, then \fRfoo/a\&.js\fP and \fRfoo/baz/a\&.js\fP are considered ignored\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBIf it matches an entry in \fRfiles\fP and also in \fRignores\fP\&.\fP For example, if \fR**/*\&.js\fP is in \fRfiles\fP and \fR**/a\&.js\fP is in \fRignores\fP, then \fRfoo/a\&.js\fP and \fRfoo/baz/a\&.js\fP are considered ignored\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBThe file is outside the \fRbasePath\fP\&.\fP If the \fRbasePath\fP is \fR/usr/me\fP, then \fR/foo/a\&.js\fP is considered ignored\&.
.PP

.PP
\fBImportant:\fP A pattern such as \fRfoo/**\fP means that \fRfoo\fP and \fRfoo/\fP are \fInot\fP ignored whereas \fRfoo/bar\fP is ignored\&. If you want to ignore \fRfoo\fP and all of its subdirectories, use the pattern \fRfoo\fP or \fRfoo/\fP in \fRignores\fP\&.
.SH "Caching Mechanisms"
.PP
Each \fRConfigArray\fP aggressively caches configuration objects to avoid unnecessary work\&. This caching occurs in two ways:

.PP
.IP "1." 4
\fBFile-based Caching\&.\fP For each filename that is passed into a method, the resulting config is cached against that filename so you're always guaranteed to get the same object returned from \fRgetConfig()\fP whenever you pass the same filename in\&.
.IP "2." 4
\fBIndex-based Caching\&.\fP Whenever a config is calculated, the config elements that were used to create the config are also cached\&. So if a given filename matches elements 1, 5, and 7, the resulting config is cached with a key of \fR1,5,7\fP\&. That way, if another file is passed that matches the same config elements, the result is already known and doesn't have to be recalculated\&. That means two files that match all the same elements will return the same config from \fRgetConfig()\fP\&.
.PP
